{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh's cup was in my hand.</b> In Bereshit Rabbah (88:5) they said: From here the Sages enacted four cups on Pesach, corresponding to the four times 'cup' is mentioned here. And one may wonder: what did the Holy One, blessed be He, show to the chief butler in this, and what does the matter of the four cups of Pesach have to do with the dream of the chief butler? And it appears to me, for it is known that the drinking of the cup signifies salvation from trouble, as it is written (Psalms 116:13), 'כּוֹס יְשׁוּעוֹת אֶשָּׂא' — 'I will lift the cup of salvation,' and the four cups of Pesach will prove it. And every captive is surrounded by the four families of evil that are mentioned in the book of Jeremiah (Jeremiah 15:2): 'כֹּה אָמַר ה׳ אֲשֶׁר לַחֶרֶב לַחֶרֶב וַאֲשֶׁר לַמָּוֶת לַמָּוֶת וַאֲשֶׁר לָרָעָב לָרָעָב וַאֲשֶׁר לַשְּׁבִי לַשֶּׁבִי' — 'Thus says HaShem: those who are for the sword, to the sword; and those who are for death, to death; and those who are for famine, to famine; and those who are for captivity, to captivity.' And in Bava Batra (8a) Rabbi Yohanan said: each one that is later in the verse is harsher than the one before it, and so on, until 'captivity' — all of them are contained within it. For it is in the master's hand to chastise him with the sword, and to put him to death, and to starve him, and to afflict him with the matter of imprisonment. And it turns out that every captive in the dungeon house, when he goes forth from the house of bondage, it is fitting that he drink four cups of salvation — for since captivity has all of them contained within it, then in his going forth from there, from the house of imprisonment, he is delivered from all four. Therefore in the chief butler the word 'cup' is mentioned four times, for He showed him in a dream that he would go forth from the dungeon house and be delivered from these four evils, and would be worthy to drink four cups of salvation. And if so, our Rabbis of blessed memory learned from here that on Pesach every person is obligated to drink four cups, for Israel were likewise captives in Egypt, and the captive has all of them contained within him; therefore it is fitting that they drink four cups of salvation corresponding to deliverance from all four, for the Holy One, blessed be He, did not redeem our fathers alone from Egypt, but us as well He redeemed with them. And this is a precious interpretation. And from here Joseph judged with his wisdom that the chief butler would go forth from the house of imprisonment and be restored to his post, but the chief baker did not see in his dream anything indicating his redemption and the ransom of his soul." "And perhaps this is the intent of our Rabbis of blessed memory, in what they expounded (Ḥullin 92a), 'וּבַגֶּפֶן שְׁלֹשָה שָׂרִיגִים' (Genesis 40:10) — three princes of nations, and so on, or Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and so on — and they were expounded in several different ways concerning the success of Israel. And what did they show to the chief butler in this? Rather, that all these are the cause of Israel's redemption from captivity, and the first redemption of Joseph was through the chief butler, and through him Joseph merited all those promises — and it is easy to understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {1 "The source cited as חולין צב is Hullin 92a; the Kli Yakar writes צב which could be read as page 92, resolved here as 92a per the standard citation of this sugya."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:48.219482891Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}